A margin analysis of 4,000 routes that led to a new price structure for the three largest customers.

The logistics group had grown through acquisitions, and each company in the group still priced its routes in its own way. Some prices followed the distance, some followed the weight, and some followed what the sales team could agree on a given day.

We built a route-level model from three years of shipment data. It showed which routes earned a good margin, which were priced below their cost, and which were used mainly to fill empty trucks. The model was simple on purpose, so that the commercial team could check every number in it.

With those results, we proposed a new price structure for the three largest customers. Each one kept the same service levels, but the price now followed the cost of the route and the volume that the customer committed to. We tested the new prices with the sales managers in two workshops before any customer saw them.

Four months after the change, the margin on the affected routes was up by 2.1 points, and the number of disputed invoices had fallen sharply. The group now reviews its routes every quarter, using the same model.
